  },
  {
    "id": "sams-club-protein-powder-orgain-organic-vegan",
    "name": "Orgain Organic Vegan Protein Powder, Vanilla Bean, 2.03 lb",
    "brand": "Orgain",
    "badge": "Best Plant-Based",
    "category": "Organic plant protein (pea, brown rice, chia)",
    "description": "Sam's Club carries Orgain's organic plant protein in a 2.74 lb club size, plus Orgain Simple and Orgain Protein + Superfoods. Per the label at the time of writing, a two-scoop serving gives 21 g of protein for about 150 calories, with no added sugar and 4 to 7 g of fiber depending on flavor; it blends pea, brown rice and chia protein. Orgain does not publish a sport certification for this product. The 2.03 lb tub on Amazon, about 20 servings, came to about $1.90 per 25 g at the price we saw.",
    "pros": [
      "The dairy-free option Sam's reliably stocks",
      "USDA Organic, no added sugar, per the label",
      "Sam's reviewers often praise the club-size value"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About $1.90 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw",
      "Two scoops for 21 g of protein",
      "Some Sam's reviewers call it gritty"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The plant-based choice at Sam's. Buy the 2.74 lb club tub in store; Amazon's smaller tub costs more per gram.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "21 g",
      "Calories": "About 150",
      "Third-party testing": "None published",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $1.90 (Amazon price seen)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00J074W7Q"
  },
  {
    "id": "sams-club-protein-powder-premier-protein-shake",
    "name": "Premier Protein 30g High Protein Shake, Chocolate, 11 oz, 12 Pack",
    "brand": "Premier Protein",
    "badge": "Closest Amazon Alternative",
    "category": "Ready-to-drink protein shake (stand-in for Member's Mark)",
    "description": "Member's Mark makes a 30 g high-protein shake, not a powder, and it is sold only at Sam's Club. Per the Sam's listing, each 11 oz Member's Mark bottle has 30 g of milk and whey protein, 160 calories and 1 g of sugar. Premier Protein is the closest match you can buy on Amazon: per its listing, 30 g of protein, 160 calories and no added sugar in an 11 oz bottle. At the Amazon price we saw, this 12-pack works out to about $1.77 per 25 g.",
    "pros": [
      "Same 30 g and 160 calorie profile as the Member's Mark shake",
      "No mixing, shelf-stable",
      "Widely sold, including at Sam's in larger packs"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About $1.77 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw, dearer than any tub",
      "Contains milk protein, so not for dairy allergies",
      "No sport certification published"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy this only if you want the Member's Mark shake and cannot get to a club. Powder is cheaper per gram.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "30 g",
      "Calories": "160",
      "Third-party testing": "None published",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $1.77 (Amazon price seen)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B008JGIZGS"
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Sam's Club Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/sams-club-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Sam's Club protein powder third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the brand, because Sam's Club has no house powder and does not certify products itself. Muscle Milk is listed by the brand as NSF Certified for Sport. Optimum Nutrition calls Gold Standard banned-substance tested and promotes Informed Choice on many flavors. RAW's isolate listing names no certifier, and Orgain publishes no sport certification. Check the certifier's database for your exact flavor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Sam's Club sell a Member's Mark protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not that we could find as of November 2026. Sam's Club's protein powder aisle is name brands such as Optimum Nutrition, Muscle Milk, RAW, Dymatize and Orgain. The only Member's Mark protein product we found is a 30 g high-protein chocolate shake in 18-packs of 11 oz bottles, with 160 calories and 1 g of sugar per bottle, per the Sam's listing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Sam's Club protein powder healthy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The main powders are ordinary, reasonably clean products. Gold Standard gives 24 g of protein in about 120 calories; Orgain is organic plant protein with no added sugar; RAW is a low-fat whey isolate. Muscle Milk Genuine has about 270 to 280 calories per serving, which suits weight gain more than dieting. People with kidney disease or other conditions should ask a doctor first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Sam's Club protein powder contain dairy or lactose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most of it does. Gold Standard, RAW isolate and Muscle Milk are milk-based, and the Member's Mark shake lists milk protein concentrate. Isolate such as RAW has less lactose than concentrate but is not lactose-free. Orgain's organic plant protein is the dairy-free choice Sam's reliably stocks. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid the dairy products entirely."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the cheapest protein powder at Sam's Club?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is usually the cheapest per gram when Sam's runs its member price near the $49.98 that deal posters have reported for the 5.47 and 5.64 lb bags. That works out to about $0.65 per 25 g. Muscle Milk Genuine, listed at $30.48 for 2.47 lb, is about $1.49 per 25 g. Prices change, so check the app."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which flavor is best at Sam's Club?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buyers most often recommend Gold Standard's chocolate flavors, including the Extreme Milk Chocolate that Sam's sells in the 5.64 lb bag, and Vanilla Ice Cream as a mixer for smoothies. RAW buyers tend to favor its dessert-style flavors. Orgain buyers are split: some like the vanilla bean, others find the chocolate gritty. These reflect buyer reviews, not our tasting."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the common complaints about protein powder at Sam's Club?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The main complaints in Sam's buyer reviews are hidden prices online, tubs that look half full on opening, recipe changes and texture. One Muscle Milk Genuine reviewer reported a changed formula and grainy texture; some Orgain buyers call it gritty; a RAW buyer questioned the serving count. Stock also varies by club, so a product online may be missing in store."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Store Brands",
    tags: [
      "sam's club protein powder",
      "protein powder sam's",
      "protein powder at sam's club",
      "member's mark protein powder",
      "raw protein powder sams"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Cheap Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-cheap-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Sam's Club Protein Powder</Fragment>
      What Sam's Club actually stocks, whether Member's Mark makes a protein powder, what each tub costs per 25 g of protein and how Sam's stacks up against Costco, Walmart and Aldi.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-sams-sells">What Sam's Sells</a>
      <a href="#members-mark">Member's Mark</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#raw">RAW at Sam's</a>
      <a href="#third-party">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#membership">Is Membership Worth It</a>
      <a href="#vs-stores">vs Costco, Walmart, Aldi</a>
      <a href="#amazon">On Amazon?</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Sam's Club does not sell a Member's Mark protein powder right now; its powder aisle is name brands, led by Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, Muscle Milk, RAW whey isolate and Orgain, and the only Member's Mark protein product is a 30 g shake. Value depends on member prices you see only when signed in. Below: the shelf, the labels, cost per 25 g and the Costco math.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Sam's Club Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sam's Club is a good place to buy name-brand protein powder in big sizes, but it has no house-brand powder: Member's Mark currently makes a 30 g protein shake, not a tub of powder. The best buy on the shelf for most people is <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard</a> in the 5.47 or 5.64 lb bag. If members see it near the $49.98 that deal posters have reported, that is about $0.65 per 25 g of protein, close to Kirkland territory at Costco.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy at Sam's if you already hold a membership and want Gold Standard, Muscle Milk, RAW or Orgain in a warehouse size. Skip it if you want a cheap own-brand whey: Costco's Kirkland, Aldi's Elevation and Walmart's Equate all exist, and Sam's has nothing like them in powder form.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product at Sam's</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sam's size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sam's price seen</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5.47-5.64 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Member price hidden ($49.98 reported by deal posters)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.65 if $49.98</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Muscle Milk Genuine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.47 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">32 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$30.48</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.49</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">RAW Whey Isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.56-1.97 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$29.98 (1.56 lb, 20 servings)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.50</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Plant Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.74 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Member price hidden</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check in club</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Member's Mark 30 g shake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 x 11 oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.48 a bottle (2024 buyer report)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.23</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Sam's Club Protein Powders and Their Amazon Equivalents
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sam's Club sells these powders in club and on samsclub.com, often in warehouse-only sizes. Each card links to the same name-brand product on Amazon (a different tub size in most cases), with the live price we saw and the cost per 25 g of protein worked out from the label. The last card stands in for the Member's Mark shake, which is a Sam's Club exclusive and not sold on Amazon.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-sams-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Protein Powders Does Sam's Club Sell?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sam's Club's protein powder range is almost entirely name brands, and the core names are Optimum Nutrition, Muscle Milk, RAW Nutrition, Dymatize and Orgain. Our research across samsclub.com listings found Gold Standard 100% Whey in a 5.47 lb Vanilla Ice Cream bag and a 5.64 lb Extreme Milk Chocolate bag, Muscle Milk Genuine and Muscle Milk Pro Series, RAW Whey Isolate, Dymatize ISO100 and ISO100 Clear, MuscleTech Grass Fed 100% Whey, Levels Grass Fed Whey, and three Orgain plant powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            The smaller and newer brands rotate. Sam's has listed MRE Lite, a whole-food protein powder in 30-serving tubs, Purely Inspired Organic plant protein, and Clean Simple Eats, a collagen-and-whey blend. These come and go by season and by club, so treat them as possible finds rather than permanent stock. The big three, Gold Standard, Muscle Milk and Orgain, are the ones that show up most consistently in the listings and in buyer reviews.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some brands people expect at Sam's we could not confirm. We found no current samsclub.com listing for Body Fortress or Garden of Life powders, even though both are easy to find at Walmart. Premier Protein is a strong presence at Sam's, but mostly as ready-to-drink shakes; if you want its powder, our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein powder review</a> covers what is in the tub. Our <a href="/body-fortress-protein-powder-review/">Body Fortress review</a> and <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life review</a> cover those brands if your club does carry them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sizes are the real difference from a supermarket. Sam's sells Gold Standard in a bag slightly larger than the standard 5 lb tub, Orgain in a 2.74 lb club tub instead of the usual 2.03 lb, and Muscle Milk Genuine in a 2.47 lb size. Bigger packs usually mean a lower price per gram, but only if you finish the bag before it goes stale and only if you like the flavor. A 5.64 lb bag holds roughly 80 scoops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Online versus in club</h3>

          <p>
            Samsclub.com shows many products as a members-only price that you see only when signed in, and prices can differ between the website and your local club. Several powders are ship-only, and some club-only items never appear online. If you are making a special trip for one product, check the app for your club's stock first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="members-mark" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is There a Member's Mark Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No, we could not find a Member's Mark protein powder on sale at Sam's Club as of November 2026. Searches of samsclub.com listings turned up name-brand powders only, and the one Member's Mark protein product that appears is the Member's Mark 30 g High Protein Shake in chocolate, sold in an 18-pack of 11 oz bottles.
          </p>

          <p>
            That shake is worth knowing about, because it is the closest thing to a Sam's Club own-brand protein. Per the Sam's listing at the time of writing, each bottle has 30 g of milk and whey protein, 160 calories, 1 g of sugar and 2 g of net carbs, with milk protein concentrate high in the ingredient list. That is the same nutrition profile as Premier Protein and Costco's Kirkland shake, and it competes with both on price.
          </p>

          <p>
            On price, the only figure we could find was a 2024 Sam's buyer review that put the shakes at about $1.48 a bottle in the 18-pack, or roughly $26.64 a case. If that still holds, 30 g of protein for $1.48 works out to about $1.23 per 25 g. That is cheap for a ready-to-drink shake but still dearer than a big bag of whey, which is the usual trade: you pay for the bottle, the liquid and the convenience. We could not confirm the current shelf price, so check your club.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why no Member's Mark powder? Sam's has not said. Costco has Kirkland whey, Aldi has Elevation and Walmart, which owns Sam's, sells Equate whey, so the gap is unusual. Until Sam's launches one, the practical answer for Sam's shoppers who want a house-brand price is to buy Gold Standard on a member deal, or to read our <a href="/kirkland-protein-powder-review/">Kirkland protein powder review</a> if you also have a Costco card.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
