        <div id="raw" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is RAW Protein Powder at Sam's Club a Good Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            RAW Whey Isolate at Sam's Club is a good isolate at a fair club price, but it is not the cheapest protein in the building. The Sam's listing for the 1.97 lb Dark Chocolate tub shows 25 servings of 25 g, and Sam's has also listed a smaller 1.56 lb Milk and Cookies tub at $29.98 for 20 servings.
          </p>

          <p>
            When RAW launched at Sam's in November 2023, Stack3d reported the 25-serving isolate at $43.98, which it said was more than $10 less than RAW's own website. At that price the tub costs about $1.76 per 25 g. On Amazon we saw the same Dark Chocolate 25-serving tub at $59.98, or about $2.40 per 25 g, so the club price, if it is still in that range, is a real saving for RAW fans.
          </p>

          <p>
            What you get for the premium over Gold Standard is a pure grass-fed whey isolate rather than a blend, with low fat and carbohydrate per scoop. Isolate carries less lactose than concentrate, which can help people who get mild bloating from cheaper whey, though it is not lactose-free. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains when that difference is worth paying for, and our <a href="/cbum-protein-powder-review/">CBUM protein powder review</a> goes through RAW's flavors and labels in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyer reviews on Sam's are mixed in the way club reviews usually are. Many buyers praise the flavors, especially the dessert-style ones. Complaints center on value: one reviewer described a container as about half full on opening, which is normal settling for powder but feels poor when the tub is large, and another questioned the serving count. If cost per gram is your priority, Gold Standard on a member deal beats RAW by more than half.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Sam's Club Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some of it is: Muscle Milk is listed by the brand as NSF Certified for Sport, and Optimum Nutrition describes Gold Standard as banned-substance tested, with Informed Choice certification promoted on many flavors. Sam's Club itself does not test or certify the powders it sells, and there is no Member's Mark powder to check.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the shelf is less clear. The RAW Whey Isolate listing we checked does not name a banned-substance certification program, and other retail listings describe it only as tested for potency and purity. Orgain does not publish a sport certification for its organic plant protein. We found no certification published for the Member's Mark shake. None of that means these products are unsafe; it means you are relying on the brand's own quality control rather than an outside certifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are a drug-tested athlete, the rule is simple: buy a product carrying NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice, and confirm the exact product, flavor and lot in the certifier's online database before you open it. At Sam's, Muscle Milk Genuine and Pro Series are the easiest route to that. Certifications are flavor- and market-specific, so a logo on one Gold Standard flavor does not cover every bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are a separate question from banned substances, and plant powders tend to test higher for lead than whey because the plants take it up from soil. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide covers what the testing programs do and do not check. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, take medication or have a dairy allergy, ask a doctor or registered dietitian before adding any protein supplement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is a Sam's Club Membership Worth It Just for Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not on protein powder alone for most people. A Sam's Club membership costs $60 a year for Club and $120 for Plus after the increase that took effect on May 1, 2026, so you need to save at least $60 a year on powder before the card pays for itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is the arithmetic. If Gold Standard costs about $0.65 per 25 g on a member deal and about $1.44 on Amazon, the saving is roughly $0.79 per 25 g. Someone drinking one 25 g shake a day uses about 365 servings a year, so the saving could be close to $290, well past the fee. Someone drinking two or three shakes a week saves closer to $100, which still covers the Club fee but not by much once you add the drive.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that the cheap figure depends on a deal price, and Sam's member prices move. Walmart sells Gold Standard and several budget whey brands with no membership at all, and Amazon Subscribe and Save sometimes narrows the gap. If you only buy powder, compare the price on the shelf that week against our <a href="/best-walmart-protein-powder/">best Walmart protein powder</a> picks before you renew.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the membership does make sense is when protein is one item among many. If you already shop Sam's for groceries and fuel, the powder savings are a bonus. Plus members also get free shipping on many orders and Sam's Cash rewards, which by 2026 had risen to a $750 annual cap, though neither changes the math on a single tub much.
          </p>
        </div>

